WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.150
- Hi all, thanks very much.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:14.010
I'll be talking today
about, as Peter said,

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:15.510
a bit of my dissertation research,

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.620
which looks at the
introduction of copyright

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.330
in the 18th century, and
copyright's influence

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:22.923
on the book market at large.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:26.790
And today, I'll be looking at patterns

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.140
in derivative artistic practices.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.480
So, text reuse, image reuse,

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:36.260
and how those sort of
patterns were massaged

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:38.723
by changes in copyright
law during the period.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:42.940
So, this is computational
research in the humanities

00:00:44.376 --> 00:00:46.220
that sort of builds on a lot of prior

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:48.530
computational research in the humanities,

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:51.260
the earliest example of which I could find

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:54.180
is work by Patricia Margaret Took,

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.806
who has this beautiful
hand-drawn distribution

00:00:57.806 --> 00:01:00.530
of the number of printers and the number

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:03.920
of books published in
the early modern period.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:05.230
So, this is back in the seventies,

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:07.010
this is in her dissertation.

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:10.230
Pioneering work for studies,
computational studies,

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:12.530
of the early book market.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:15.420
Later work by folks like John Feather

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:18.060
were analyzing distribution over topics

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.800
within books published in this period.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:23.673
Subsequent work by Alain Veylit.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.160
Starting to compute some
of the sort of basic

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:30.120
descriptive statistics of books published,

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.823
let's say before 1800, in
the early modern period.

00:01:33.756 --> 00:01:37.720
And then, more recent work
by folks like Michael Suarez,

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:39.900
who, again, here he's
looking at distribution

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.850
of book lengths over history.

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:46.272
But there's been very little
analysis of the ways in which

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:49.130
copyright law may have
influenced the book market.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:51.000
In fact, there's been
very little quantitative

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.470
work done generally in the humanities.

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:55.950
Humanities papers tend to be
presented in tweed jackets

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.060
and read from a sheet of paper.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.810
There's very little computing going on.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:03.630
So, to set the stage
for what we'll discuss,

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.320
I'm just going to sort of
walk through the relevant

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:08.223
historical moments very briefly.

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:12.210
Copyright's invented in 1710,
statutory copyright anyway,

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:13.830
which is sort of written law copyright.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:17.090
Before that, it's all royal proclamations.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:20.810
And so, after 1710, there
are a number of cases

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:24.020
that are fought over derivative works,

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:26.190
where an author is
abridging, plagiarizing,

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:28.874
adapting an earlier existing work.

00:02:28.874 --> 00:02:32.360
And the plaintiff wins
in all of these cases.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:35.333
The defendant is unable to
defend a derivative publication.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.780
Fast forward to 1741,

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:42.710
and there's a very prominent
case, Gyles v. Wilcox,

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:44.990
which is fought over a publication

00:02:46.118 --> 00:02:48.260
by Fletcher Gyles, that we just saw.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:51.690
So, John Wilcox had basically abridged

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:54.320
Gyles' text, and published it.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:58.380
And, Gyles was understandably upset.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:00.690
He took Wilcox to court,

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:03.200
and Wilcox defended his
publication on the basis

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.950
that he had highly
manipulated his source text.

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.250
He had transformed it in a number of ways,

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:11.460
he wasn't going to influence

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.762
the marketability of the
original text, et cetera.

00:03:14.762 --> 00:03:17.330
And so, his derivative
publication was identified

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.441
as a distinct copyrightable work

00:03:19.441 --> 00:03:22.350
and was found non-infringing.

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:24.460
And so, this precedent in 1741

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:28.050
opens up the opportunity for
a number of subsequent cases,

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:32.350
all of which have derivative
artistic text reuse

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:35.330
that are defensible, that
are found non-infringing.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:38.060
So, this is a really sort of
watershed moment in history.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:38.980
I mean, this is the creation

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:41.603
of fair use copyright law in 1741.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:44.390
At least for texts.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:48.200
In the image domain, there
was no fair use law until

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:53.200
a very sort of breakthrough,
1785 case, Sayer v. Moore.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.810
And, these are the actual images

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:58.023
that were disputed in that case.

00:03:59.028 --> 00:04:02.760
On the left, an image
of this sort of eastern

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:05.510
sea coast by Robert Sayer.

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:08.530
And, on the right, you can
look at just the left half

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:11.850
of this image, on the same
by John Hamilton Moore.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:13.810
And, if you kind of squint at it,

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:15.990
you can see the coloration's
very different, obviously,

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:18.560
but the figuration is highly similar.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:21.883
And, in fact, Moore's maps
were based on Sayer's.

00:04:23.284 --> 00:04:27.420
And this was proven in the case.

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:31.419
It's discovered that
certain misidentified labels

00:04:31.419 --> 00:04:34.970
in the Sayer map were
actually also transcribed

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:38.083
into the Moore map, and that
becomes like an artistic trick.

00:04:38.083 --> 00:04:41.330
Artists would deliberately
in code false information

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:44.583
into their pictorial graph so
that if they were reprinted,

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:48.580
they could identify those
telltale marks, kind of clever.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:51.830
But, in any event, because
this was a sea chart,

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:56.050
and because Moore's
map, the derivative map,

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:59.316
made a number of improvements
on the plaintiffs' map,

00:04:59.316 --> 00:05:01.230
his map was actually found non-infringing.

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:04.220
It was found to be a defensible work.

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:06.377
And so this creates precedent

00:05:06.377 --> 00:05:08.896
for a number of subsequent cases,

00:05:08.896 --> 00:05:12.846
all of which revolve around
derivative image production,

00:05:12.846 --> 00:05:17.846
where a derivative image is
found to be non-infringing.

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:23.134
And so the research question
that this part of my work

00:05:23.134 --> 00:05:25.587
sort of addresses is,

00:05:25.587 --> 00:05:30.587
"Does the rise of fair use
precedent incentivize, increase,

00:05:30.987 --> 00:05:33.200
"rates of derivative artistic practice?"

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.320
So, once you get a
precedent for text reuse

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:37.750
that says you can infringe
on other people's works,

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:41.410
and your work will be defensible,
it will be monetizable,

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:46.360
do we see rises in sort of
frequency of text-imagery use?

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:48.400
And, a number of prominent scholars

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:50.210
have sort of contributed to this debate.

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:52.020
Essentially, Ronan Deazley,

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:55.170
professor of law has said essentially yes.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.490
And William St. Clair,
whose one of the few

00:05:57.490 --> 00:05:59.677
who's tried to actually grab some numbers

00:05:59.677 --> 00:06:04.530
to analyze this question, he
said no, he doesn't think so.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:07.214
But St. Clair is operating on the order

00:06:07.214 --> 00:06:11.360
of just about a dozen cases
that he's kind of analyzing.

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:15.020
So, what I want to do is
to address this question

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:18.020
sort of at the level of collections.

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:19.380
And the data for this comes from,

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:21.180
actually, two distinct repositories.

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:25.601
There's a great collection
of 18th century books,

00:06:25.601 --> 00:06:28.520
called Eighteenth Century
Collections Online,

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:30.373
those are the red dots down here.

00:06:31.213 --> 00:06:36.104
And, we can measure the
degree to which that sample

00:06:36.104 --> 00:06:39.690
represents the full corpus size,

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:44.690
the full population, by comparing
the ECHO publication stats

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:47.320
to the English Short Title Catalog.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:51.500
The latter, the ESTC, is a bibliography

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:54.910
of all texts known to have
been published in English

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:58.430
from 1472, the introduction
of printing, to 1800.

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:01.730
So, it's an absolute sort
of historical backbone

00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:04.420
against which we can measure
representativeness of a corpus,

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:05.900
which is very rare.

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:08.845
In more modern times, we have
no idea of the full print run

00:07:08.845 --> 00:07:12.890
of a given historical
sweep, it's just too much.

00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:16.600
But we can see here that ECHO's
sort of highly correlated.

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:18.610
ECHO publication rates
are highly correlated

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:20.130
with the ESTC, so this is a highly

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:22.693
representative corpus sample.

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:26.640
So, here's a sample page image from ECHO.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:29.880
This is a page image, page
scan, that has an image,

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:31.130
obviously, and some text.

00:07:31.977 --> 00:07:34.640
This is kind of a sample
ballad, a little poem form.

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:37.004
And, so this one of roughly 35 million

00:07:37.004 --> 00:07:42.004
page scans in all of the ECHO corpus.

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:46.393
All of which will be analyzed
in the work that follows.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:50.560
And, there are number of
problems with this data.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:53.440
One of which is that we can
measure representativeness

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:57.280
of the ECHO corpus versus the
English Short Title Catalog,

00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:58.770
but we don't know what's missing

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:00.510
from the English Short Title Catalog.

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:02.510
We don't know the survival rates of books.

00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:05.670
A lot of books have kind of
just disappeared from history.

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:07.810
And, there's actually very little work

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:10.740
on measuring probability that...

00:08:12.224 --> 00:08:14.770
a given work would have
disappeared or that's measuring

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:16.413
survival rates of books.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:19.620
And so, if we look at the records

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:21.150
of English Short Title Catalog,

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:25.020
which are gathered from over
2200 libraries worldwide,

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:27.770
we can look at the sort
of percent of books

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:30.210
overtime that are held
in just a single library,

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:33.558
that were basically on the
verge of becoming extinct.

00:08:33.558 --> 00:08:37.225
And, you can see it's up to
60% in the very early period,

00:08:37.225 --> 00:08:38.190
and things kind of dip down.

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:41.460
So, a significant portion of book history

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:45.122
is just absolutely missing and
can't be part of the study,

00:08:45.122 --> 00:08:49.423
but we've got decent
numbers that have survived.

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:52.840
Geographically, there are problems, too.

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:55.050
The vast majority of publishing
was happening in London,

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:57.373
that's this sort of
bleeding heart down here.

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:03.257
Whereas later in the century,
the later the period,

00:09:03.257 --> 00:09:06.060
we get a lot of publishing
in Edinburgh and Dublin,

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:07.930
which are kind of hotbeds of reprinting,

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:08.880
because they weren't subject

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:11.720
to English copyright law permissions.

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:14.420
So, printers in Edinburgh and Dublin

00:09:15.421 --> 00:09:18.150
can reprint sort of wholesale,

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:20.380
they can sell records in London.

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:24.110
They deliberately misidentify title pages,

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:26.630
they lie and say that a record
was published in London,

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:31.373
because London publications
were sort of hotter commodities.

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:35.170
And then there are kind of intricacies

00:09:35.170 --> 00:09:37.870
within these different
geographic locations.

00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:39.100
So, for instance, if we look

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:41.673
at optical character recognition quality

00:09:41.673 --> 00:09:45.440
over the 18th century, in
these different cities,

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:48.521
we can see that London had nicer type,

00:09:48.521 --> 00:09:52.430
it tended to be preserved
a bit better on the page,

00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:54.280
and so the OCR rates were higher.

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:55.920
Whereas a lot of Dublin printers

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:59.463
are working with secondhand
presses, they have old typeface,

00:09:59.463 --> 00:10:02.010
and this sort of character...

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:03.910
The imprint quality, the actual ink

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:06.500
pressed on the page, is much lower.

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:08.880
So, although Edinburgh and
Dublin are kind of, again,

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:12.570
these hotbeds of reprinting,
we can't capture as much

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:15.253
from them because the
signal is less strong.

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:19.123
So, a lot of problems
with the actual data.

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:23.270
That said, we'll begin
by looking at patterns

00:10:23.270 --> 00:10:25.850
in derivative text reuse.

00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:28.450
And, as we've observed,

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:30.720
this is all optical
character recognition data,

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:31.890
so these are page scans

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:34.623
from which we've inferred
a character sequence.

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:39.196
And, OCR rates are
variable over the period.

00:10:39.196 --> 00:10:41.760
We can see that sort of mean line there,

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:45.210
drawn roughly in the 80% region.

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:48.710
So, this is to say that
the text reuse algorithm

00:10:48.710 --> 00:10:51.476
needs to be sufficiently
fuzzy in order to work

00:10:51.476 --> 00:10:54.520
with these character irregularities.

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:56.960
We can't count on perfect

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:59.363
sort of word level transcription rates.

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:04.630
And so, the technique used
for this part of the study

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:08.340
is called Minhash Algorithm,
which allows us to analyze

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:13.500
text sequences and identify these hashes,

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:14.852
these sort of...

00:11:14.852 --> 00:11:17.510
Derive these characteristics of the text,

00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:20.730
such that we can compare the
characteristics of two passages

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:23.501
and identify with a certain probability,

00:11:23.501 --> 00:11:26.400
whether or not they match.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:29.400
And so, the basic notion here
is that we're going to take

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:31.870
each of those 35 million page scans,

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:32.810
we're going to break it

00:11:32.810 --> 00:11:35.440
into a sequence of multi-word windows.

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:36.900
For each of those multi-word windows,

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:37.870
we're going to break that

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:41.420
into a number of character sequences,

00:11:41.420 --> 00:11:42.660
re-character sequences.

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:46.551
So, REP, first three letters, EPO, POR,

00:11:46.551 --> 00:11:47.384
so, we have the subsequent

00:11:47.384 --> 00:11:50.760
sliding window going over characters.

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:53.600
We're going to quantify each
of these character sequences,

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:56.360
and then we're going to take
this sort of quantifiable

00:11:56.360 --> 00:11:58.579
representation of the character sequence,

00:11:58.579 --> 00:11:59.412
and we're going to use that

00:11:59.412 --> 00:12:03.309
to update a global vector for this window.

00:12:03.309 --> 00:12:05.760
For this vector, it's just
a list of any numbers,

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:09.150
and that is a parameter
of the Minhash Algorithm.

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:12.410
So, this might be 128 set values,

00:12:12.410 --> 00:12:17.410
and we're going to use this
value module 128 hash functions,

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:20.500
which is just to say kind of clock time,

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:23.380
but if you hit 12, you go back to one.

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:25.340
So, we're going to be
cooking these numbers

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:29.350
by virtue of this number, just
like a clock, essentially.

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:31.510
Each of these is going to
update this little vector,

00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:32.343
and at the end of the day,

00:12:32.343 --> 00:12:35.364
we're going to pass a sliding
window over this vector,

00:12:35.364 --> 00:12:37.570
and we're going to take
each of those sequences,

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:40.470
and add this record to the list of records

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:42.770
in which that sequence of values

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:45.913
occurs in the vector, for the window.

00:12:46.840 --> 00:12:50.603
So, this lets us analyze
text reuse in linear time,

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:52.710
which is much better than comparing

00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:56.660
35 million page images to
35 million page images.

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:59.530
It's nice to have that optimization.

00:12:59.530 --> 00:13:02.030
And so, with this sort
of Minhash technique,

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:05.470
we can identify fuzzy text reuse,

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:08.230
such as the passages here.

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:09.760
We have a recipe.

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:11.100
It's interesting to look at the kinds

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:12.170
of things that are plagiarized.

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:14.320
Recipe books, song books,
are often plagiarized.

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:17.560
Legal books, often plagiarized.

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:18.793
It's highly ironic.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:22.710
And so, we'll get a chance
to see some of the actual

00:13:22.710 --> 00:13:25.835
OCR underlying some of this in a second.

00:13:25.835 --> 00:13:27.200
But, this is the kind of fuzzy text reuse

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:28.450
we're trying to identify.

00:13:30.145 --> 00:13:34.090
Then, of course, the vast
majority of the aligned passages,

00:13:34.090 --> 00:13:36.350
the identified instances of text reuse

00:13:36.350 --> 00:13:38.980
are solely a function edition reprints.

00:13:38.980 --> 00:13:41.570
So, we have three different
editions of Robinson Crusoe,

00:13:41.570 --> 00:13:44.070
there are 120 something odd...

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:46.210
And, in fact, if we look at reprints

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:47.930
over the course of the century,

00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:49.870
almost half of publishing
that was happening

00:13:49.870 --> 00:13:51.340
during the period was just reprints

00:13:51.340 --> 00:13:53.710
of a given successful volume.

00:13:53.710 --> 00:13:56.426
So, we have to use elaborate measures

00:13:56.426 --> 00:13:58.790
to sort of construct this big graph

00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:01.477
and connect each of the edition reprints

00:14:01.477 --> 00:14:04.750
with connected components,
that allow us to identify

00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:07.737
that this region is all Robinson Crusoe,

00:14:07.737 --> 00:14:11.003
whereas this is an actual
plagiarism of Crusoe.

00:14:12.430 --> 00:14:13.590
So, if we flush all of this out,

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:15.670
we remove those edition reprints,

00:14:15.670 --> 00:14:19.560
and we analyze the actual
rates of derivative publication

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:22.200
over the period, we get
these pretty smooth curves.

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:25.303
We can see that sort
of reprinting behavior

00:14:25.303 --> 00:14:27.680
grows throughout the century

00:14:27.680 --> 00:14:29.810
but then kind of reaches
a saturation point.

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:31.960
It's like almost a market saturation,

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:34.630
and then there's no subsequent growth.

00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:38.120
But, we don't see a big spike after 1741,

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:42.780
after Gyles v. Wilcox,
which we would expect to see

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:47.253
had Gyles v. Wilcox influenced
derivative text publication.

00:14:49.004 --> 00:14:51.640
So, so far it looks like the rise

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:52.980
of fair use in the text domain

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:56.050
has not influenced text
reprinting practices.

00:14:56.050 --> 00:14:57.003
What about images?

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:01.390
Well, here's just the
sort of image corpus,

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:03.580
how many images are
being printed over time.

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:05.230
Several thousand annually,

00:15:05.230 --> 00:15:07.373
and these are the different image types.

00:15:08.540 --> 00:15:11.612
Okay, in the sort of processing pipeline,

00:15:11.612 --> 00:15:14.480
there are number of challenges posed.

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:19.060
One of which is the sort of
variance within exposure rates.

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:21.594
So, these are two different images,

00:15:21.594 --> 00:15:25.660
two different page scans,
of the same edition.

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:27.890
And, you can see on the left, obviously,

00:15:27.890 --> 00:15:30.543
we have far less signal
than the one on the right.

00:15:31.870 --> 00:15:34.040
There are these strange eccentricities

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:36.590
of the corpus, as well,
where sometimes two pages

00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:40.560
will be kind of consolidated
into a single page image.

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:42.200
So, these are distinct pages,

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:46.040
this right-side page
is one composite image

00:15:46.040 --> 00:15:48.180
that somehow represents the two images.

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:51.310
It's almost like an automatic
page flipper got stuck.

00:15:51.310 --> 00:15:52.143
Not sure.

00:15:53.450 --> 00:15:56.730
Other strange sort of figures
where it looks like someone's

00:15:56.730 --> 00:15:59.050
gone in with scissors
and they particularly

00:15:59.050 --> 00:16:01.850
like this woman, so they just cut her out.

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:04.530
Pasted on some 18th
century wall somewhere.

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:07.840
So, this is a sort of
reprinting of the image

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:12.650
but there's very little signal
there to capture that with.

00:16:12.650 --> 00:16:15.940
And then, there's the more
sort of philosophical problem,

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:18.600
which is that you can have two images

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:20.655
which are highly similar,

00:16:20.655 --> 00:16:23.990
and then it becomes
almost a probability game.

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:26.360
What is the probability of simultaneous

00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:27.990
co-discovery of these two images?

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:30.340
What's the probability that
these images were generated

00:16:30.340 --> 00:16:32.446
independently of each other?

00:16:32.446 --> 00:16:34.042
Just by virtue of the fact

00:16:34.042 --> 00:16:36.930
that this is a large space,

00:16:36.930 --> 00:16:39.070
and there will be random occurrences,

00:16:39.070 --> 00:16:43.170
where two authors will
generate the same image.

00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:47.140
And so, even deciding in a number of cases

00:16:47.140 --> 00:16:49.580
whether or not two images are derivative

00:16:49.580 --> 00:16:52.983
can be difficult, I think, for humans.

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:58.010
So, when we run this image computation,

00:16:58.010 --> 00:17:00.830
and I'll say really
briefly that this is...

00:17:00.830 --> 00:17:04.893
I had the most baroque
labyrinthine pipeline for this.

00:17:06.202 --> 00:17:08.750
This involved using sort of
vectorized image representations

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:12.930
from inception on key-point
based image analyze techniques,

00:17:12.930 --> 00:17:16.491
like SIFT and SURF,
image hashing techniques,

00:17:16.491 --> 00:17:20.120
like a perceptual hash, an average hash,

00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:21.780
combining all of those features together,

00:17:21.780 --> 00:17:24.240
and then actually just using an old school

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:27.630
support vector machine
classifier, given that feature.

00:17:27.630 --> 00:17:32.470
And that was trained on 300000
observations, image pairs,

00:17:32.470 --> 00:17:35.770
that were binary classified
as matching or not matching.

00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:37.540
I'm happy to share that training data

00:17:37.540 --> 00:17:39.964
that was very painful to collect,

00:17:39.964 --> 00:17:42.703
with the jankiest web
app you've ever seen.

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:47.290
Using that data, we can sort
of analyze all these images,

00:17:47.290 --> 00:17:49.010
and we can do a number of things.

00:17:49.010 --> 00:17:50.380
In the first place, we can identify

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:52.290
kind of editorial practices.

00:17:52.290 --> 00:17:56.010
So, these are three different page samples

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:57.743
from the Champions of Christendom,

00:17:58.630 --> 00:18:01.790
and you can see that the
authors kind of changed his mind

00:18:01.790 --> 00:18:04.096
about the skeleton, he didn't
quite like this skeleton,

00:18:04.096 --> 00:18:04.929
so he just keeps upgrading

00:18:04.929 --> 00:18:07.790
this skeleton in each of the editions.

00:18:07.790 --> 00:18:09.560
Kind of interesting.

00:18:09.560 --> 00:18:10.950
But we can also identify

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:14.420
a number of very subtle plagiarisms,

00:18:14.420 --> 00:18:16.580
where an engraver was actually referring

00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:19.400
to a source image, and
sort of re-crafting it.

00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:24.220
And so, I spent weeks analyzing
these kinds of image pairs,

00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:26.084
and playing spot the difference,

00:18:26.084 --> 00:18:29.079
trying to find exactly
where the subtleties are.

00:18:29.079 --> 00:18:31.237
But if you look at the
different groups of people,

00:18:31.237 --> 00:18:33.642
there are two people sitting here,

00:18:33.642 --> 00:18:36.930
and they're slightly different
when moving over here.

00:18:36.930 --> 00:18:39.770
This was a kind of regular occurrence,

00:18:39.770 --> 00:18:43.891
where an author, an engraver,
would hire someone else

00:18:43.891 --> 00:18:47.730
to sort of re-craft, re-imagine an image.

00:18:47.730 --> 00:18:52.260
So, this is a perfectly derivative image,

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:55.018
published by a different
author, a different publisher

00:18:55.018 --> 00:18:56.350
than the original source image.

00:18:56.350 --> 00:18:59.100
And we can identify these on that scale.

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:02.031
On the left, this is an
image by Thomas Jeffreys

00:19:02.031 --> 00:19:04.380
and on the right, this is actually

00:19:04.380 --> 00:19:06.550
a publication by Edmund Burke.

00:19:06.550 --> 00:19:07.790
As you can see, it's kind of flushed

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:09.760
out the Jeffreys' image.

00:19:09.760 --> 00:19:11.870
It's ironic because
Jeffreys was the defendant

00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:14.220
in a number of image dispute cases

00:19:14.220 --> 00:19:16.290
where he was actually plagiarizing others.

00:19:16.290 --> 00:19:18.910
So, there may be a sort of history here,

00:19:18.910 --> 00:19:20.160
where Jeffreys is plagiarizing

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:22.083
and Burke is plagiarizing on top.

00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:27.500
But if we look at the rates of image reuse

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:29.957
over the full sweep of the century,

00:19:29.957 --> 00:19:33.290
we can see it's very
minimal in the first place.

00:19:33.290 --> 00:19:38.150
In fact, only about half
of 1% of book publications

00:19:38.150 --> 00:19:42.750
contains derivative images,
contains plagiaristic images,

00:19:42.750 --> 00:19:44.250
over the sweep of the century.

00:19:45.190 --> 00:19:48.340
And there's no noticeable spike

00:19:48.340 --> 00:19:52.130
after the Sayer v. Moore case in 1785.

00:19:52.130 --> 00:19:55.067
So, to return to the original question,

00:19:55.067 --> 00:19:56.817
"Does the rise of fair use precedent

00:19:56.817 --> 00:19:58.880
"increase derivative artistic practices?"

00:19:58.880 --> 00:20:03.346
It seems William St. Clair
was right to think not.

00:20:03.346 --> 00:20:04.903
Thanks very much.

00:20:04.903 --> 00:20:07.903
(audience applause)

00:20:11.439 --> 00:20:13.856
(soft music)

